= Director General of Hydrocarbons
<director-general-of-hydrocarbons>

== Origins and Establishment
<dgh-origins>

The office has two distinct institutional antecedents:

+ *The Dasgupta Committee.* A committee under Dr. A.B. Dasgupta, reviewing
  Bombay High reservoir management, first recommended an autonomous
  conservation board to enforce sound reservoir engineering practice.

+ *The Kaul Committee (1992).* A committee under Shri P.K. Kaul, former
  Cabinet Secretary, was constituted in 1992 to examine the restructuring
  of ONGC's organisational structure. It recommended establishing an
  independent regulatory body to handle leasing and licensing, safety and
  environmental oversight, and reservoir and conservation management.

The rationale for independence was structural: these functions could not
credibly remain inside ONGC once ONGC itself became a competitor for
blocks.

The Directorate General of Hydrocarbons (DGH) was accordingly established
in 1993, under the administrative control of the Ministry of Petroleum and
Natural Gas, by Government of India Resolution. It was *not* created by a
standalone parliament-enacted statute. This is why the DGH is often
described as a technical advisory body rather than a statutory regulator
in the strict sense --- a distinction that matters when comparing it with
the PNGRB, which does rest on a statute.

== What the DGH Actually Does
<dgh-functions>

+ *Technical advisory role.* Advises the Ministry on the exploration and
  optimal exploitation of hydrocarbons; reviews the exploration
  programmes of PEL-holders under the Oilfields Act and the PNG Rules
  1959, and advises government on their adequacy.

+ *Reserve and development oversight.* Evaluates discovered and estimated
  hydrocarbon reserves; advises on acreage offers and relinquishment;
  reviews field development plans and proposed exploitation rates.

+ *Policy implementation.* The DGH has been the nodal agency implementing
  both NELP and HELP/OALP. It monitors Production Sharing Contracts and
  Revenue Sharing Contracts, and tracks the reservoir performance of
  producing fields.

+ *Data custodian.* Maintains the National E&P Data Base and Archive
  System and the National Data Repository (NDR) --- a genuinely national
  asset, since old seismic and well data acquires new value as processing
  technology improves.

+ *Field presence.* DGH geophysicists and scientists are stationed on
  seismic vessels and deepwater drilling rigs during live operations, and
  accompany raw seismic data whenever it leaves the country for
  processing.

+ *Funding.* The DGH is funded through Oil Industry Development Board
  (OIDB) grants, for which it presents annual budgetary estimates.


= NELP
<nelp>

== Background and Objectives

+ Before 1991--92, petroleum exploration and production was carried out
  only by the public sector oil companies, such as ONGC and Oil India
  Limited (OIL). A New Exploration and Licensing Policy (NELP) was
  formulated in 1997 for the exploration and production of crude oil and
  natural gas (excluding CBM --- coal bed methane).

+ The main objective was to attract significant risk capital from Indian
  and foreign companies, state-of-the-art technologies, geological
  concepts, and best international practices for the exploration and
  production of oil and gas resources in the country.

+ A Director General of Hydrocarbons, established in 1993, was appointed
  as the nodal agency for the new NELP policy.

+ A further boost to the sector came when exploration and production was
  opened up to private and foreign investment, with 100% FDI allowed
  under the rubric of the NELP scheme.

== Key Features of NELP
<key-features-of-nelp>

+ *Foreign investment.* FDI of up to 100% is allowed under NELP.

+ *Competitive bidding.* Blocks are awarded through an open international
  competitive bidding mechanism. NELP operated on periodic discrete
  bidding rounds (NELP-1 through NELP-19), and companies had to wait for
  the Ministry to officially release a set of predefined blocks during a
  specific window.

+ *Level playing field.* ONGC and OIL are placed at the same level as
  private companies and must compete for petroleum exploration licences
  on a competitive basis, rather than obtaining them on a nomination
  basis as before. They also receive the same fiscal and contractual
  terms as private companies.

+ *Marketing freedom.* Contractors may freely market their crude oil and
  gas in the domestic market, although export of oil and gas is not
  permitted.

+ *Two-tiered royalty.* Royalty is set at 12.5% for onland areas and 10%
  for offshore areas, to incentivise offshore exploration. The offshore
  rate extends to twelve nautical miles from the coastline of India.

+ *No mandatory state participation.* There is no mandatory state
  participation through ONGC or OIL, and no carried interest of the
  State.

  Under a carried interest arrangement, the government or the state-owned
  company receives a free percentage share in an oil block. The private
  company bears 100% of the capital investment required for drilling,
  survey and exploration; if no oil is found, the private company loses
  its money while the government is owed nothing. Conversely, if oil is
  found, the private company covers the upfront cost and gradually
  recovers the expense out of future oil sales, and once the venture
  reaches a profit-making stage the profit is split between the State and
  the private entity.

  The objective is to allow the State to take a share in a successful
  project without bearing high upfront financial risk. Before NELP, ONGC
  and OIL were granted oil fields on a nomination basis and held
  mandatory stakes or carried rights in the blocks, allowing them a split
  of the profit without the excessive upfront financial overhead. To
  create a level playing field, the automatic free equity stakes granted
  to ONGC and OIL were no longer valid.

+ *Deep-water royalty relief.* Royalty was charged at half the prevailing
  rate for companies engaged in deep-water mining beyond 400 metres, for
  the first seven years after commencement. Mining at a depth of 400
  metres and above is harder and more expensive than drilling near the
  shore; the exception therefore enabled companies to recover their large
  setup costs and overheads.

+ *Cess exemption.* Cess was exempted for production from blocks offered
  under NELP.

+ *Import duty exemption.* Companies were exempted from payment of import
  duty on goods imported for petroleum operations.

+ *No signature, discovery or production bonuses.* Companies need not pay
  extra upfront or performance-based cash payments to the government ---
  such as a payment upon signing a contract, upon discovery, or once oil
  and gas production reaches a specific volume target. The focus is again
  to remove the upfront financial burden on private and foreign
  companies, making the hydrocarbon sector attractive to explore.

= Issues with NELP
<issues-with-nelp>

== Fragmented Policy Framework

There are separate policy regimes for conventional oil and gas, coal bed
methane, shale oil and gas hydrates. Different fiscal terms are also in
force for the allocation of acreages and for different hydrocarbons. In
practice, however, resources overlap between different contracts.
Unconventional hydrocarbons such as shale gas and shale oil --- natural
gas and liquid crude oil trapped under organic formations called
"shale" --- were unknown when NELP was framed.

The fragmented policy framework led to inefficiencies: upon exploring one
type of hydrocarbon, if another is found it requires separate licensing,
which adds to the overhead cost.

== The Production Sharing Contract
<the-production-sharing-contract>

NELP rested on production sharing contracts (PSCs). Under a PSC the State
retains ownership of the hydrocarbon in the ground; the contractor bears
the exploration risk and the upfront capital, and is repaid out of the
production itself. The mechanism works in two stages:

+ *Cost oil.* The contractor first recovers its exploration and
  development costs out of a share of the crude oil or gas produced.

+ *Profit oil.* Whatever remains after cost recovery is the "profit oil",
  which is split between the contractor and the government in an agreed
  ratio.

The consequence is that the government's revenue depends on the
contractor's _profit_, and profit can only be measured once costs are
known. The State therefore had to verify the contractor's costs rather
than simply tax its output. This is the structural reason the DGH exists
as a cost-verification and monitoring body: the PSC model made an
approving authority unavoidable.

That verification architecture generated three distinct problems:

+ *Discretion and delay.* The government created a system of stage-wise
  approvals, without which activities could not commence. This vested
  wide discretion in the approving authority and became a major source of
  delay and dispute, as the government and the contractor clashed over
  whether a particular cost was necessary and whether it had been
  correctly stated.

+ *Revenue leakage.* Because the government's share arises only after
  costs are recovered, the contractor has an incentive to inflate costs
  or to show losses. In some cases corporations reported inordinate
  losses on exploration and production that were in substance devices to
  evade profit sharing.

+ *Adversarial relationship.* The need to audit every cost turned the
  State and the contractor into parties in a permanent accounting
  dispute, rather than partners in a venture.

This cost-recovery logic is precisely what HELP (2016) abandoned in
favour of revenue sharing, under which the government takes a share of
gross revenue from the outset and the contractor's costs are no longer
audited by the State.

== Restricted Access to Acreage

Exploration was confined to blocks put to tender by the government.
Private entities might hold blocks they would like to pursue for
exploration on the basis of their own information or interest, but this
was an untapped opportunity until the government brought those blocks to
bidding. There were thus "fixed bidding windows."

== Administered Gas Pricing

The pricing of gas, especially the producer price of gas, was fixed
administratively by the government. In most cases the government set a
price lower than actual market value, which led to lower corporate
revenue and therefore lower taxes and royalties. Although freedom to
price oil and gas in the domestic market was stated, that freedom was
subject to a price ceiling imposed by the government. Where the global
market was soaring, the ceiling prevented production companies from
taking advantage of the high prices; where global prices crashed, the
applicable price was below the ceiling.


= Land Rights Angle
<land-rights-angle>

Exploration and production rights under NELP concern the hydrocarbon in
situ. Getting that hydrocarbon to market requires a separate and
overlapping set of rights over the _surface_ --- the land on which
pipelines, terminals and storage facilities are built. Two routes exist:
private negotiation and statutory acquisition under the PMPA Act.

== Private Negotiation: Easement and Right of Way

Even before any notification for acquisition is issued, private pipeline
entities may approach landowners to enter into "easement" or "right of
way" agreements. This route is attractive to developers partly because
the PMPA Act caps statutory compensation at 10%. That cap does not sit
well with landowners, which is why developers also pay _ex gratia_
compensation through private channels to secure consent.

== Statutory Acquisition: The PMPA Act 1962
<the-pmpa-act-1962>

The Petroleum and Minerals Pipelines (Acquisition of Right of User in
Land) Act 1962 (PMPA Act) provides the framework governing the
acquisition of a right of user in land for laying pipelines for the
transportation of natural gas, and matters connected therewith. It sets
out the acquisition procedure, the restrictions on the use of the land,
and the compensation payable to persons interested in the land.

=== The Acquisition Procedure

The procedure runs in stages:

+ *Declaration of intention.* The federal government may declare its
  intention, by notification, to acquire the land where it considers it
  necessary in the public interest for pipelines to be laid for the
  transport of natural gas.

+ *Objection.* Any person interested in the land has a right to make an
  objection within 21 days of the date of the notification.

+ *Declaration in the Official Gazette.* After objections (if any) are
  resolved, the federal government may declare in the Official Gazette
  that the right of user in the land may be acquired. The right over the
  land then vests in the federal government, and may be passed on to the
  state government or to any other corporation or entity.

+ *Compensation.* Any person interested in the acquired land is entitled
  to compensation payable under the PMPA Act.

For LNG storage facilities forming part of an LNG terminal, the developer
may instead purchase or lease the land, or enter into concession
agreements where the land is owned by the government.

=== Restrictions on Use and Compensation

The acquisition does not bar all use of the land. *Section 9* preserves
the landowner's right to use the land for other purposes, except the
construction of buildings or wells, or the planting of trees.

*Section 10* governs compensation, which is determined by the
jurisdictional District Judge. In assessing it, the Judge considers:

+ the value of the cut down trees or crops;
+ the value of the temporary severance of the land; and
+ the possible costs of injury that may be caused to the land in the
  process.

If compensation is not paid, interest accrues at 6% per annum.

= HELP
<help>

The Hydrocarbon Exploration and Licensing Policy (HELP) was notified in
2016, replacing the NELP regime. Its provisions are drawn from the HELP
policy notification. HELP rests on four structural changes: a single
licence for all hydrocarbons, open acreage access, a revenue sharing
fiscal model, and a graded royalty regime.

== Single Licence for Conventional and Non-conventional Hydrocarbons
<single-licence>

A uniform licence enables E&P operators to explore and extract all
hydrocarbon resources covered under the Oilfields Regulation and
Development (ORD) Act 1948 and the Petroleum and Natural Gas (PNG)
Rules 1959. The licence permits the contractor to explore both
conventional and unconventional oil and gas resources --- including CBM,
shale gas/oil, tight gas, gas hydrates, and any other resource identified
in future that falls within the definition of "Petroleum" and "Natural
Gas" under the PNG Rules 1959.

This directly answers the fragmentation defect of NELP, under which a
separate licence was required for each hydrocarbon type found in a block.

== Open Acreage Licensing Policy (OALP)
<oalp>

E&P companies may submit offers for blocks at any time by filing an
initial Expression of Interest (EOI) indicating the area, in terms of
grids, that they wish to take up. Modalities for operationalising OALP
are notified separately.

=== How OALP Operates

+ *Data access.* A prospective bidder first purchases and studies
  geological and seismic data. This is possible because of the National
  Data Repository (NDR), launched alongside OALP as a necessary
  complement: companies needed to see subsurface data before deciding
  which blocks to carve out.

+ *Expression of Interest.* Having identified an area of interest, the
  bidder carves out a block and submits an EOI. The block is then put out
  for competitive auction, open to other bidders.

+ *Weightage to the EOI submitter.* The original EOI-submitter receives a
  small additional weightage at the technical evaluation stage, as a
  reward for having identified the opportunity. This is a "right of first
  offer"-type advantage, not exclusivity.

+ *Bid evaluation.* Financial bids are evaluated on two co-equal
  parameters --- the revenue share offered to government and the biddable
  Minimum Work Programme committed to --- each carrying equal weight. This
  is a deliberate design choice, ensuring the government does not optimise
  for revenue share alone at the expense of actual exploration effort.

+ *"No-Go" area liberalisation.* Progressively larger shares of India's
  sedimentary basins previously kept off-limits ("No-Go" areas, largely
  for defence and environmental reasons) have been opened for bidding.
  OALP Round IX (2025), for instance, covered 136,596 km#super[2] across
  eight basins, of which 38% lay in previously restricted No-Go areas.

+ *Rounds conducted.* Nine-plus OALP rounds had been conducted by 2025,
  progressively awarding blocks. By one account, four rounds under HELP
  had together awarded 94 blocks as of late 2020, with activity
  continuing well beyond that.

OALP was formally launched in July 2017, ending a seven-to-eight-year gap
since the last NELP round (2010).

== Fiscal Regime
<fiscal-regime>

The production sharing fiscal system, based on Pre-Tax Investment
Multiple (PTIM) and cost recovery or production-linked payments, is
replaced by a revenue sharing model. The contractor pays a biddable
Government share of revenue, net of royalty, under a Revenue Sharing
Contract (RSC).

== Royalty
<royalty>

Royalty rates for onland blocks are 12.5% for oil and 10% for gas, at par
with NELP. To incentivise offshore exploration, which involves higher
risk and cost, a graded system of reduced royalty rates applies. Shallow
water blocks attract a flat 7.5% for both oil and gas. Deep water and
ultra-deep water blocks are treated more generously still: no royalty is
payable for the first seven years, after which the rate is 5% for deep
water and 2% for ultra-deep water, for both oil and gas:

