== Pricing and Sale of Crude Oil
<pricing-crude-oil>

The contractor is free to sell crude oil exclusively in the domestic
market, through a transparent bidding process on an arm's length basis.
For the calculation of Government revenue, however, the minimum price is
the price of the Indian Basket of Crude Oil --- currently comprising Sour
Grade (the average of Oman and Dubai) and Sweet Crude (Dated Brent)
processed in Indian refineries --- as calculated monthly by the Petroleum
Planning and Analysis Cell (PPAC). If the price arrived at through
bidding exceeds the Indian Basket price, the Government's take is
calculated on the actual price realised.

== Pricing and Sale of Natural Gas
<pricing-natural-gas>

The contractor has freedom of pricing and marketing of gas produced from
these blocks on an arm's length basis. For the calculation of Government
revenue, the minimum price is the price calculated under the Domestic
Natural Gas Pricing Guidelines in vogue at the relevant time. If the
price discovered on an arm's length basis exceeds the price calculated
under those Guidelines, the Government's take is calculated on the actual
price realised.

== Sale of Condensate
<sale-of-condensate>

All the provisions governing the sale of crude oil apply _mutatis
mutandis_ to condensates.

== Increased Exploration Phase
<increased-exploration-phase>

The exploration period is 8 years for onland and shallow water blocks. To
encourage exploration in deepwater, ultra-deepwater and frontier blocks,
the exploration period is 10 years.

== No Restriction on Exploration Activity during Contract Period
<no-restriction-on-exploration>

The contractor is allowed to carry out exploration activity during the
entire contract duration. Exploration is at the sole risk and cost of the
contractor.

== Model for Inviting Bids
<model-for-inviting-bids>

Blocks are awarded on the basis of 100 points, allocated between the
revenue share offered to the Government and the committed biddable
Minimum Work Programme (MWP), each carrying a weightage of 50 points.
Bidders bid the percentage of revenue they will share with the
government, and the model operates on a revenue-based linear scale. The
contractor is required to pay the biddable Government share of revenue,
net of royalty (that is, post-royalty).

== Empowered Committee of Secretaries
<empowered-committee-of-secretaries>

An Empowered Committee of Secretaries (ECS) is constituted, consisting of
the Secretary, Petroleum and Natural Gas, the Finance Secretary and the
Law Secretary. Its functions are:

+ approval of Model Revenue Sharing Contracts and other bid documents;
+ resolution of all contractual issues;
+ resolution of inter-ministerial coordination issues; and
+ approval of the modalities for operationalising OALP.

== Custom Duty and Oil Cess
<custom-duty-and-oil-cess>

Exemption from custom duty is provided on all machinery, plant,
equipment, materials and supplies related to petroleum operations. There
is no levy of cess on crude oil.

== Management Committee (MC)
<management-committee>

A Management Committee is constituted with representatives from the
Government and the Directorate General of Hydrocarbons (DGH) on the one
hand, and the contractor on the other. Its role relates largely to
monitoring the Minimum Work Programme and technical aspects.

== Eligibility for Participation in Bids
<eligibility-for-participation>

Foreign and Indian companies may hold 100% participating interest.
Participation of the Government, or of Government nominee companies, is
not required in any joint venture.

== Site Restoration
<site-restoration>

Abandonment and site restoration of a field or block is governed by the
Government of India's guidelines, rules and regulations in vogue from
time to time. The provisions of the notification came into force with
immediate effect and remain in force until further orders.

