    table.header([*Category*], [*Definition*]),
    table.hline(),
    [Category I], [Basins which have reserves and are already producing],
    [Category II], [Basins which have contingent resources pending
     commercial production],
    [Category III], [Basins which have prospective resources awaiting
     discovery],
  )]
  , kind: table
  , caption: [Basin categories under HELP]
)

Category I basins are the mature, producing basins. Categories II and III
are progressively less explored, and the fiscal regime is progressively
more generous to compensate for the higher risk.

== Category II and III Concessions
<category-ii-iii-concessions>

For blocks in Category II and III basins, the following concessions apply:

+ *No revenue share bidding.* Revenue share is not a bidding parameter,
  except where a windfall gain arises. Revenue share becomes payable only
  where revenue exceeds USD 2.5 billion.

+ *Extended and phased exploration.* Exploration is extended and phased,
  with bidding confined to 2D and 3D seismic work. Swapping of Common
  Work Programme (CWP) with other surveys is permitted.

+ *Increased originator incentive.* The originator incentive is raised to
  10 marks for Category II and III basins.

+ *Concessional royalty for early production.* A concessional royalty
  rate applies where commercial production begins early.

= The Licensing and Leasing Framework
<licensing-and-leasing-framework>

Under the ORD Act 1948 and the PNG Rules 1959, the State's mineral rights
to oil and natural gas are transferred to participants through an auction
process. Two distinct instruments are used:

+ *Licence (Petroleum Exploration Licence, PEL).* Granted for the
  exploration and appraisal stage. A licence does _not_ grant any
  interest in the oil and gas itself.

+ *Lease (Petroleum Mining Lease, PML).* Granted for the development and
  production stage. A lease grants the participant an interest to
  extract, utilise or sell hydrocarbons.

