== Legal Status of the Rights
<legal-status-of-rights>

The rights held by a participant are both *contractual* and *regulatory*
in character. They are contractual because they arise under the PSC or
RSC and the licence or lease. They are regulatory because certain terms
of the licence or lease are embodied in the PNG Rules 1959, and because
the rights are subject to pervasive government control.

== The Management Committee
<management-committee-help>

PSCs and RSCs provide for a Management Committee (MC), consisting of
representatives of the State and the contractor. Under the pre-NELP and
NELP regimes, the MC approved work programmes and budgets, field
development plans, and abandonment and site restoration. With the advent
of HELP, and to promote ease of doing business, the MC's role has been
gradually reduced to that of an observing and advisory body only. This is
the institutional counterpart of the shift from cost-recovery to revenue
sharing: once costs are no longer audited, an approving committee has
little left to approve.

= State Value Extraction
<state-value-extraction>

The State derives economic value from oil and gas development through
five principal channels:

+ *Royalty.* Rates range from 2% to 12%, depending on the basin category
  and the onshore or offshore status of the block. Concessional rates
  apply where production begins early.

+ *Production or revenue share.* Under the PSC regime, participants bid
  the government's profit share, and cost recovery was allowed, so that
  participants could deduct exploration costs before determining profit
  petroleum. Under the RSC regime, the government takes a share of
  "Revenue", defined as all amounts accruing to the contractor, net of
  taxes and duties (except corporate income tax) and after subtraction
  of royalty for that month.

+ *Taxes.* Corporate income tax at 30% under the Income Tax Act 1961.
  Expenditure on unsuccessful exploration is allowable. In the case of
  production, 100% of exploration expenditure may be deducted in the
  first year, or amortised over ten years.

+ *Surface rent.* A nominal deposit and yearly rent is payable to the
  government as licence or lease fees.

+ *Domestic sale obligation.* All crude oil and natural gas produced must
  be sold domestically in India. This is not a revenue channel as such,
  but it is how the State converts the sector into energy security.

== Low and High Revenue Points
<low-and-high-revenue-points>

Under the RSC, the revenue share varies with the level of revenue
achieved. The government specifies two reference points when inviting
offers:

+ *Low Revenue Point (LRP)* and *High Revenue Point (HRP).*

The revenue share is a biddable range, expressed as a percentage of total
revenue at the LRP and at the HRP. Revenue share for intermediate points
is calculated by linear interpolation. The bidder offering the highest net
present value of revenue share to the Government receives the maximum
marks under this parameter.

= Transfer, Pledge and Security of Interests
<transfer-pledge-security>

== Consent for Transfer
<consent-for-transfer>

The consent of the DGH is required for the transfer of oil and natural gas
development interests. Consent is not unreasonably withheld, but the
government considers the technical and financial capability of transferees
before granting it. Consent is usually refused where sanctions or public
policy considerations apply to a potential transferee.

For assignment to affiliates, no technical or financial assessment is
required, but parent company guarantees are required. An assignment or
transfer may not be made where the participating interest retained by the
assignor, or the percentage interest of the assignee, would be less than
10% of the total participating interest of all participants --- except
where the government, on the recommendation of the MC, permits otherwise
in special circumstances. Change of control is subject to the same
procedure.

== Pledging and Accounting
<pledging-and-accounting>

Rights granted under a PSC or RSC may be pledged for security or booked
for accounting purposes under domestic law, subject to three conditions:

+ the participant remains liable for its obligations under the PSC or
  RSC;
+ the pledge is subordinated to the rights of government under the PSC
  and the rights of other consortium members under Joint Operating
  Agreements; and
+ prior government consent is required for the list of lenders to be
  considered for creating such a pledge.

== Security and Guarantees
<security-and-guarantees>

Participants must provide security and guarantees:

+ *Bank guarantee to the DGH* for an amount equivalent to their
  participating interest share of liquidated damages for exploration. If
  the block progresses to development, bank guarantees may extend up to
  5% of the total expenditure proposed for development, depending on the
  peculiarities of the block.
+ *Parent company guarantee to the DGH* for assurance of performance.
  Where there is no parent company, the participant itself must provide
  the guarantee.

Standard formats of these guarantees are annexed to the model contracts
and provided at the start of a bidding round.

== Currency and Fund Remittance
<currency-and-fund-remittance>

There are no additional restrictions on fund remittances in the oil and gas
sector beyond the standard regulations applicable to other industries. The
sector allows 100% FDI through the automatic route. However, companies
based in nations bordering India face stricter regulatory scrutiny and
must obtain prior government approval for any investment, regardless of
the sectoral cap or the automatic route available to other investors.

= Site Restoration and Decommissioning
<site-restoration-and-decommissioning>

Site restoration and abandonment are governed by guidelines issued by the
DGH. The framework operates as follows:

+ *Approval.* The abandonment plan must be submitted to and approved by
  the DGH through the Management Committee.

+ *Additional scrutiny.* The plan must be submitted to the Oil Industry
  Safety Directorate (OISD) for offshore production sites and to the
  Directorate General of Mines Safety (DGMS) for onshore production
  sites. It must also be submitted to other authorities, such as pollution
  control boards, which may provide advisory comments.

+ *Timing.* A high-level schedule of the abandonment plan must be
  submitted to the DGH one year prior to the estimated cessation of
  production. The final plan must be submitted to the OISD or DGMS no
  later than one year after cessation of production.

+ *Funding.* Under the Site Restoration Fund Scheme 1999, participants
  must constitute a fund maintained with the State Bank of India to fund
  estimated decommissioning costs. The fund is opened immediately after
  first commercial production. Annual contributions are made on a unit of
  production method, based on the reserve-to-production ratio of the
  field. Contributions are reviewed every three years by an independent
  third party, and withdrawals are permitted to pay abandonment costs.

= Other Government Authorisations
<other-authorisations>

Beyond exploration and production authorisations, developing oil and gas
reserves requires multiple further approvals:

+ *Environmental Clearance (EC).* Required under the Environment
  (Protection) Act 1986 and the EIA Notification 2006, involving an EIA
  study and public consultation. Granted by the Ministry of Environment,
  Forest and Climate Change and the State Environment Impact Assessment
  Authority.

+ *Consent to Establish and Consent to Operate.* Required under the Water
  (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act 1974 and the Air (Prevention
  and Control of Pollution) Act 1981, from the respective State Pollution
  Control Boards.

+ *Coastal Regulation Zone clearance.* Required under the CRZ
  Notification 2019 where the project is located near coastal areas.

+ *PESO approval.* Required from the Petroleum and Explosives Safety
  Organisation for storing petroleum exceeding 2,500 litres.

+ *Labour and safety compliance.* The Mines Act 1952 regulates the health
  and safety of workers, overseen by the DGMS, which inspects mines,
  investigates accidents and grants statutory permissions and exemptions.

= Downstream and Midstream Regulation
<downstream-midstream-regulation>

== The PNGRB
<pngrb>

Under the PNGRB Act 2006, the Petroleum and Natural Gas Regulatory Board
regulates the refining, processing, storage, transportation, distribution,
marketing and sale of petroleum, petroleum products and natural gas. Any
entity wishing to market, establish or operate natural gas or petroleum
terminals, or to establish storage facilities, must obtain prior
authorisation.

== Adani Gas v. PNGRB (2021)
<adani-gas-v-pngrb>

*Citation note.* The Supreme Court determined whether the scope of
"deemed authorisation" under the PNGRB Act extends to providers such as
Adani who had received _provisional_ licences from the State. The decision
was rendered in the aftermath of the seven-Judge reference which held that
CNG regulation falls within the absolute domain of the Union.

=== Facts

+ The Board gave provisional authorisation to Adani for Ahmedabad and
  Dascroi, excluding certain disputed areas.

+ Adani applied for the disputed areas, but its application was rejected
  on the ground of delay and laches. At the stage of the application, the
  PNGRB sought various details, which were not furnished to the Board's
  satisfaction.

+ Adani moved the Gujarat High Court, challenging the denial of the grant.
  The High Court observed that while Adani had challenged Regulation 18,
  it had itself obtained authorisation in Lucknow under Regulation 18.
  Its challenge to the regulation was therefore based on convenience.

+ The High Court also held that the idea of deemed authorisation is for
  those who had obtained prior authorisation from the Central Government
  --- for instance, under the Petroleum Act 1937. It could not have applied
  to those who had obtained prior authorisation from State authorities,
  since the States had no jurisdiction, as the Reference had observed.

=== The Legislative Competence Holding

It was decided in the Reference that the Union would have sole legislative
competence to regulate CNG. The production of natural gas is not
independent of the production of any other petroleum product. This being
so, under Entry 53, List I, the Union alone has competence to regulate
natural gas.

State Entry 25 of List II contains "gas and gas works." The Court held
that the phrase cannot be read in an isolated manner: "gas" and "gas
works" must be read together, so that only that artificial gas which
requires "gas works" may be included in that legislative field.

=== The Nature of the PNGRB Act

The Act does not deal with the extraction of PNG. It deals with
downstream activities such as the storage, processing and transport of
petroleum and natural gas. These were previously regulated by a variety of
regulations promulgated by the States. To develop a uniform mechanism, the
PNGRB Act was enacted --- though at the time, Section 16 had not yet been
brought into force.

The reason for the delay in enforcing Section 16 was practical: had it been
brought into force immediately, all distribution networks functioning prior
to the enforcement of the Act would have stalled, and would first have had
to obtain permission from the Board. A grace period was therefore given for
developers to take steps toward registration, while allowing their
pipelines and distribution infrastructure to continue in use or under
construction.

=== Deemed Authorisation under Section 16

It was argued that the deemed authorisation under the Section would apply
to all those who had been undertaking the functions provided for under
Section 16 before the appointed date of its enforcement. Adani argued that
this provision allowed it to continue in the absence of an authorisation
given under Section 17.

The Court, however, having read the statute in full, identified that the
deemed acceptance under Section 16 is subject to the provisions of "this
Chapter," which also includes Section 17, concerning the registration of
entities functioning as a carrier or CGD prior to the enactment of the
PNGRB Act.

The appellants cited a prior Supreme Court decision in _Adani Gas_, which
had held that the deemed authorisation provision is concerned with those
who had obtained authorisation from the State. The Court found this to be
incorrect, since the seven-Judge Reference had already declared that the
States had no power to legislate on the subject: their authorisation,
granted under a prior enactment, would be void and inoperative. The focus
of the statute is not to retroactively revive those authorisations.

Per contra, the Court overturned the previous _Adani Gas_ judgment and held
that the deemed authorisation provision is for applicants that had already
obtained approval from the Central Government. Section 17 clearly indicates
this distinction: those who had obtained prior authorisation from the
Central Government were merely required to _intimate_ the Board of certain
details, while those who had not obtained any authorisation needed to
_register_ themselves with the PNGRB. The Court found this difference
between intimation and registration to be crucial. Thus, the NOC obtained
by Adani from the State to continue operations is of no consequence insofar
as Section 16 is concerned.

=== Challenge to Regulation 18

It was argued that Regulation 18 is _ultra vires_ since it provides no
guidelines for the exercise of executive discretion by the PNGRB in
granting or rejecting an application under Section 17. The issue arose from
the framing of Regulation 18, which stated that the PNGRB "may" consider
the factors laid down from (a) to (j). This "may," it was argued, gave the
PNGRB arbitrary powers.

The Court held that the challenge fails. Regulation 18 is meant to be a
uniform standard, but the discretion exercised by the PNGRB is essential to
the feasibility of the policy concerned, as it applies to the entire
nation. The phrase "may take into consideration" is neither a mandatory
rule nor a discretionary rule in the general sense. In determining an
application, the appropriate weightage given to each factor depends on the
"totality of all facts and the composite nature of and interlinkages
within existing criteria." This does not render the power arbitrary. It may
subsequently be exercised in an arbitrary manner, to which judicial review
is available, but the regulation itself is _intra vires_ the Act.

Crucially, empowering an expert body to issue appropriate guidelines and
notifications is not beyond the competence of subordinate legislation.

=== Approbate and Reprobate

The Court refused to hear arguments against the validity of the
authorisation given to Adani by the Board. Adani had challenged the
authorisation to the extent that three disputed areas had been incorrectly
left out.

The Court held that one cannot challenge a decision from which one has
enjoyed an advantage. Adani, having enjoyed the effect of the authorisation
in Ahmedabad and Dascroi, could not challenge it by writ in respect of the
disputed territories: it is deemed to have acquiesced in the exclusion of
those areas. Further, if any aspect of it were to be challenged, Adani
could have used the statutory appeal mechanism. Adani's choice not to
exercise its option to appeal against the PNGRB order regarding the
disputed areas was a crucial factor keeping the Court from examining the
factual assertions made by the appellant.

=== Why the Case Matters

+ *Federal competence.* It settles that CNG and natural gas regulation is
  exclusively a Union subject under Entry 53, List I, and narrows State
  Entry 25 ("gas and gas works") to artificial gas requiring gas works.

+ *Scope of the PNGRB Act.* It confirms the Act is a downstream statute:
  it does not govern extraction, but storage, processing and transport.

+ *Deemed authorisation.* It overrules the earlier _Adani Gas_ position and
  confines Section 16 to those holding Central Government authorisation,
  distinguishing intimation from registration under Section 17.

+ *Delegated legislation.* It upholds Regulation 18, holding that
  conferring structured discretion on an expert regulator is not excessive
  delegation, and that arbitrariness in a particular exercise is a matter
  for judicial review rather than a ground of _ultra vires_.

+ *Approbate and reprobate.* It applies the doctrine to bar a party that
  has enjoyed the benefit of an authorisation from challenging the same
  authorisation in respect of excluded areas.

== PNGRB v. Indraprastha Gas
<indraprastha-gas>

The Delhi High Court held that the PNGRB does not have the power to
regulate the Maximum Retail Price (MRP) at which petroleum and natural gas
is to be sold by entities such as Indraprastha to final consumers. To the
extent the PNGRB Regulations provide for such price regulation, they are
_ultra vires_. Having regard to Section 11 of the Act, these forms of price
regulation are not within the functions of the PNGRB, and are thus beyond
the competence of the Board.

=== Definitions in the Act

+ *City or local gas distribution network.* A network of gas pipelines and
  the associated equipment required to transport petroleum and natural gas
  from a bulk high-pressure transmission line to a medium-pressure
  distribution grid, and finally to the end customer.

+ *Common carrier.* A pipeline for the transportation of petroleum or
  petroleum products by more than one entity on a non-discriminatory,
  open-access basis. It does _not_ include pipelines which transport these
  products to a specific consumer, or for the supply of crude oil.

+ *Contract carrier.* A pipeline for the transport of petroleum and natural
  gas by more than one entity pursuant to firm contracts for at least one
  year, as may be declared or authorised.

=== The Regulatory Boundary

The Board clearly has power to declare an existing city gas distribution
network as a common or contract carrier for the purposes of the Act.
Section 21 allows for a right of first use, whereby an entity has the right
to use its own equipment for its own requirement before letting out the use
of the remaining capacity to other entities. For those using an existing
distribution network, the Act provides for payment of a transport tariff
under Section 22.

The question that arose was whether this allows the Board to set the
transport tariff even where the transport services are availed in a
_captive_ form.

It was submitted that the transportation tariff provision begins with the
phrase "subject to the provisions of the Act," which means that at least in
respect of common and contract carriers generally, the power to levy and
regulate the levy of transport tariff exists. The issue was whether the
High Court was right in saying that a class of consumers --- captive
consumers --- cannot be regulated by the Board.

While Section 11 allows the PNGRB to regulate various aspects of the
distribution mechanism, such as transport and city or local distribution
networks, the High Court observed that this power is limited to regulating
the _inter se_ relationship between entities under the Act, and not their
relation with the end consumer. Only where the Board has found that
distributors have formed a cartel, or are indulging in retrograde
practices, can the Board intervene. The regulation concerns the conduct of
the entity when it acts as part of the pipeline, or as a contract or common
carrier --- not the consumers. This observation was accepted by the Court.

=== Casus Omissus

The Court also found that the absence of any provision allowing the PNGRB
to regulate pricing or transport tariff in respect of captive consumption is
a _casus omissus_, in the sense that it is a deliberate exclusion. Counsel
submitted that the omission is not conscious but merely incidental. The
Court was unimpressed, since the legislative intent was manifest.

=== Section 61 Read with Section 11

Section 61, which enables the PNGRB to make regulations, must be read
together with Section 11, which clarifies that where there is no power to
regulate, there is no power to enact legislation either. The rules, having
no ground in the powers and functions of the Board under Section 11, must
be held _ultra vires_ the statute.

The power to make regulations is granted in general terms, yet the powers
of the Board enumerated in Section 11 provide the specific outer limits to
the exercise of that jurisdiction.

Accordingly, the appeal against the decision of the High Court was
dismissed.

=== Why the Case Matters

+ *Limits of Section 11.* The Board's enumerated functions are the outer
  limit of its regulatory competence; Section 61 cannot be used to expand
  them.

+ *Entity-to-entity, not entity-to-consumer.* The PNGRB regulates the
  _inter se_ relations between entities in the network, not the price paid
  by the end consumer.

+ *Captive consumption excluded.* Price and transport tariff regulation do
  not extend to captive consumption, and the omission is a deliberate
  _casus omissus_, not an oversight.

+ *Delegated legislation.* Regulations made without a foundation in
  Section 11 are _ultra vires_, however generally the regulation-making
  power is worded.

== Pipelines and Third-Party Access
<pipelines-and-third-party-access>

+ *Authorisation.* The PNGRB (Authorising Entities to Lay, Build, Operate
  or Expand Natural Gas Pipelines) Regulations 2008 establish a structured
  bidding process, initiated either by expression of interest or _suo
  motu_ by the Board.

+ *Common carrier and contract carrier.* The PNGRB may declare a pipeline
  as a common carrier. Extra capacity must be offered to third parties on
  an open access, non-discriminatory basis, allocated on a first come,
  first served basis. Access is contingent on the existence of surplus
  unutilised capacity; a customer cannot compel the operator to expand.

+ *Tariffs.* Tariffs are regulated by the PNGRB and are not freely
  negotiable. The PNGRB (Determination of Natural Gas Pipeline Tariff)
  Regulations 2008 govern gas pipeline tariffs. In March 2023 the PNGRB
  introduced the Unified Tariff System, advancing the "One Nation, One
  Grid, One Tariff" initiative. Petroleum product pipeline tariffs are
  governed by separate 2024 regulations. Tariffs are fixed on a zonal
  postalised basis and recovered from all customers without premium or
  discount.

+ *Ownership concentration.* Around 75% of operating natural gas pipelines
  in India are owned and controlled by GAIL, the state-owned gas company.

== City Gas Distribution
<city-gas-distribution>

The PNGRB grants city gas distribution (CGD) licences for geographical
areas through competitive bidding, considering criteria such as the number
of households, network coverage and proposed investment. During the
exclusivity period, the authorised entity must add additional domestic
piped natural gas connections on demand where the consumer is within 25
metres of the metering unit. After exclusivity ends, the network is
declared a common carrier and tariffs are governed by the 2020 CGD tariff
regulations.

== LNG Facilities
<lng-facilities>

LNG terminals are owned and operated by both private and government
entities. The PNGRB (Eligibility Conditions for Registering Liquefaction
of Natural Gas Terminals) Rules 2012 did not mandate registration but did
mandate providing common carrier capacity. The Draft PNGRB (Registration
for Establishing and Operating LNG Terminals) Regulations 2024 would
mandate registration and regulate third-party access. India has seven
operational LNG terminals, all onshore, with an operational regasification
capacity of around 170 MMSCMD.

== Gas Trading
<gas-trading>

India has established a market for natural gas trading through the Indian
Gas Exchange (IGX). The PNGRB (Gas Exchange) Regulations 2020 regulate
trading platforms, setting standards for registration, price discovery and
dispute resolution. No single entity other than a member of the exchange
may hold more than 25% of the paid-up equity share capital of a gas
exchange after five years from authorisation. Membership is divided into
trading members, clearing members, trading-cum-clearing members and
proprietary members. Entities intending to establish a gas exchange must
obtain authorisation from the PNGRB.

== Downstream Oil
<downstream-oil>

Oil products such as motor spirit, high speed diesel and LPG are
"essential commodities" under the Essential Commodities Act 1955. The
central government may regulate their production, supply and distribution
by issuing control orders, and may order the takeover of any petrol pump
without an opportunity of hearing to ensure uninterrupted supply. The
Petroleum Products (Maintenance of Production, Storage and Supply) Order
1999 and the Motor Spirit and High Speed Diesel (Regulation of Supply,
Distribution and Prevention of Malpractices) Order 2005 operate under this
Act. Crude oil is a derivative commodity under the Securities Contract
Regulation Act 1956 and is traded on the Multi Commodity Exchange, regulated
by SEBI.

= Competition and Dispute Resolution
<competition-and-dispute-resolution>

== Competition
<competition>

The Competition Act 2002, read with the Combination Regulations 2011,
governs competition in the sector. The Competition Commission of India
(CCI) is the sole statutory authority. The Act regulates horizontal
agreements (between competitors) and vertical agreements (between persons
at different levels of the production chain). Certain horizontal
agreements are presumed to cause an appreciable adverse effect on
competition (AAEC); the presumption does not apply to vertical agreements,
which are permitted unless an AAEC is established.

Following the Competition (Amendment) Act 2023, companies with significant
business operations in India must notify the CCI of combinations exceeding
INR 20 billion. The CCI's timeline to form a prima facie view was reduced
from 30 to 15 working days, and the overall review period from 210 to 150
calendar days.

== Dispute Resolution
<dispute-resolution>

+ *Under PSCs and RSCs.* Disputes are typically resolved by _ad hoc_,
  non-institutional arbitration. Where the participant is a government
  entity, a special mechanism applies under the Department of Public
  Enterprises, involving an arbitration panel of government
  representatives; the Arbitration and Conciliation Act 1996 does not
  apply to that procedure.

+ *Under the PNGRB Act.* The PNGRB decides disputes relating to refining,
  processing, storage, transportation, distribution, marketing and sale,
  unless the parties have agreed to arbitration. Appeals lie to APTEL
  under the Electricity Act 2003, and finally to the Supreme Court.

+ *International arbitration.* India signed the New York Convention on
  10 June 1958 and ratified it by enacting the Foreign Awards (Recognition
  and Enforcement) Act 1961, later repealed by the Arbitration and
  Conciliation Act 1996, which now governs enforcement of foreign awards.
  India is _not_ a party to ICSID.

+ *Sovereign immunity.* There is no sovereign immunity in India. The
  Constitution permits both central and state governments to be sued, and
  the Supreme Court has held that government liability under its contracts
  is akin to that of a private party.

+ *Notable precedent.* In the "Gas Migration" case, a consortium of
  foreign companies was alleged by the government to have no right to gas
  that migrated from an adjacent block. The arbitral tribunal held in
  favour of the consortium; the government's challenge was dismissed by
  the Delhi High Court and the award was upheld.

= GAIL (India) Ltd. v. Indian Petrochemicals Corp. Ltd. (2023)
<gail-v-ipcl>

*Citation.* (2023) 2 S.C.R. 326; Civil Appeal Nos. 3504--3505 of 2010;
decided 8 February 2023 by Sanjay Kishan Kaul and Abhay S. Oka, JJ. The
appeal lay from the Gujarat High Court's order of 17 June 2008 in LPA Nos.
1622 of 2006 and 1012 of 2007.

The Supreme Court held that a writ petition under Article 226 was
maintainable against a State instrumentality even in a purely commercial
contract, where the State had exercised unequal bargaining power and
levied a charge that was _ex facie_ discriminatory. The clauses levying
"loss of transportation charges" were quashed, but the refund was
restricted to three years before the filing of the writ petition.

=== Facts

+ On 1 January 1999, the Ministry of Petroleum and Natural Gas (MoPNG),
  the allocating and price-fixing authority for natural gas, issued a
  letter allocating 0.85 MMSCMD of semi-rich gas to IPCL on a firm basis
  from Hazira to IPCL's Gandhar Unit, for extraction of C-2 and C-3
  fractions.

+ The allocation was subject to two conditions: signing a gas supply
  contract with GAIL, and laying IPCL's _own_ pipelines to transport the
  semi-rich gas from Hazira to Gandhar and to transport the lean gas back
  to Hazira. IPCL was required to enter into the contract within 60 days,
  failing which the allocation would lapse.

+ IPCL set up a plant at Gandhar at a cost of approximately Rs. 4,500
  crores, and laid pipelines between Hazira and Gandhar at a cost of
  approximately Rs. 354 crores. It entered into the gas supply contract
  with GAIL on 9 November 2001.

+ Under the contract, GAIL received gas from ONGC at Hazira, and the gas
  was transported to IPCL's Gandhar plant through pipelines laid by IPCL.
  The unutilised gas was returned to Hazira, also using IPCL's pipelines.
  IPCL's pipelines alone were used; GAIL's HBJ pipeline was not used at
  all.

+ GAIL nonetheless levied "loss of transportation charges" under Clauses
  4.04 and 10.01 of the contract, on the footing that transportation
  charges were payable along the HBJ pipeline system.

+ IPCL challenged these clauses only on 9 March 2006, after five years. In
  the interim, IPCL had ceased to be a public sector undertaking, 26% of
  its shares having been sold to Reliance Petroinvestments Ltd. in June
  2002 under the Government's disinvestment policy.

+ GAIL stopped levying the charges in May 2016. The total amount collected
  under the clauses was approximately Rs. 134 crores before they were
  quashed by the Single Judge and sustained by the Division Bench.

=== The Three Questions

The Court framed the dispute within three parameters:

+ whether the writ petition challenging Clauses 4.04 and 10.01 was
  maintainable;
+ assuming it was, whether the High Court could invalidate the clauses on
  the ground of unequal bargaining power and arbitrariness or unfairness;
  and
+ whether monetary relief by way of refund could be granted after the
  order of 19 September 2006 was passed.

=== Maintainability of the Writ Petition

GAIL, through the Solicitor General, contested maintainability on the
grounds that the contract provided for arbitration before the Permanent
Machinery of Arbitrators in the Bureau of Public Enterprises under Clause
13.1; that the matter was purely contractual, involving the enforceability
and validity of contractual terms, with no case made out for violation of
fundamental rights; that the presence of a public law element was a
_sine qua non_ for writ jurisdiction, as held in _Joshi Technologies
International Inc. v. Union of India_; and that the petition was an
attempt to bypass the law of limitation, having been filed five years
after the contract was signed.

The Court held the writ petition maintainable:

+ GAIL is a Public Sector Undertaking and thus qualifies as "State" under
  Article 12 of the Constitution.

+ At the time of entering into the contract, GAIL enjoyed a monopolistic
  position with respect to the supply of natural gas in the country.

+ IPCL, having incurred significant expense in setting up the appropriate
  infrastructure, had no choice but to enter into the agreement with GAIL.
  There was thus a clear public element in the dealings between the
  parties.

+ Writ jurisdiction can be exercised where the State, even in its
  contractual dealings, fails to exercise a degree of fairness or
  practises discrimination.

+ GAIL's levy of "loss of transportation charges" was _ex facie_
  discriminatory, since IPCL was mandated to build its own pipeline under
  the allocation letter and was not using GAIL's HBJ pipeline at all.

+ Merely because an alternative remedy was available, the Court should not
  decline jurisdiction under Article 226 and relegate the parties to a
  civil remedy.

=== Validity of the Clauses

The Court held that it would be extremely unfair and unjust, apart from
being an arbitrary action in violation of Article 14, that IPCL be charged
for loss of transportation charges when it was mandated to lay its own
pipelines and not to transport gas through the HBJ pipeline. The action
also violated the principle of non-discrimination under Article 14: IPCL,
using its own pipelines, was being treated at par with other commercial
entities carrying gas through GAIL's HBJ pipeline. This was more so when
the MoPNG's pricing orders stipulated a fixed price for natural gas.

The Court accepted that once GAIL had laid down the pipeline, it was
entitled to structure its cost into the contract. But the issue was not
simply that. Two public sector enterprises had entered into a contract in
pursuance of an allocation made by the MoPNG, and there was a time
constraint on IPCL. Having incurred heavy expenditure on the Gandhar
Plant, IPCL had very little choice but to enter into the contract. Most
significantly, IPCL was bound to follow the allocation terms provided by
the principal authority, the MoPNG. IPCL was thus faced with a "Hobson's
choice": either give up the contract, or accept the clauses levying
transportation charges.

On a conspectus of these factors, GAIL had exercised unequal bargaining
power at the time of signing the contract. The contractual exercise of
providing such a clause ran contrary to every commercial and common sense
and was manifestly arbitrary, since IPCL was not being charged under any
general terms but for a specific purpose --- a purpose which could not
exist in the contract in view of the master authority, the Union of India,
providing to the contrary. GAIL's contention that the charges could be
levied merely because it had laid the HBJ pipeline for users generally was
rejected.

=== Refund and Limitation

The direction for refund, made by order dated 11 April 2007, arose as a
consequence of the quashing of the clauses. It was in the nature of a
_sequitur_, and the Court found no reason to interfere with it.

On quantum, however, the Solicitor General's alternative argument
prevailed. The issue of loss of transportation charges had been flagged by
IPCL in various communications exchanged between the parties after the
contract was signed, but that could not grant IPCL a licence to approach
the court as and when it considered proper. While upholding the quashing
of the clauses, the Court restricted the refund to a period of three years
prior to the date of filing of the writ petition, on account of IPCL's
delay in approaching the court. It drew support from _Lipton India Ltd. v.
Union of India_, where the writ petition was entertained because of the
plea of discrimination but the relief was restricted to what would have
been claimed in a suit.

=== Disposition

The appeals were partly allowed. The appeal was dismissed as to the
maintainability of the writ petition and the quashing of the clauses
dealing with loss of transportation charges. The refund was restricted to
three years before 9 March 2006, the date of filing of the writ petition,
to be made within two months, failing which it would carry interest at 8%
per annum from the date it became due. Parties were left to bear their own
costs.

=== Why the Case Matters

+ *Writ against the State in commercial contracts.* A writ petition is
  maintainable even in a purely commercial contract where the State
  instrumentality enjoys a monopoly and the contract is not a free
  bargain. The presence of a public law element, not the subject matter of
  the contract, is the test.

+ *Unequal bargaining power.* The Court looked beyond the formal equality
  of two public sector enterprises to the substantive constraint imposed
  by the MoPNG's allocation letter, which left IPCL with a "Hobson's
  choice."

+ *Article 14 in contract.* Charging a party for a service it was
  prohibited from using, and thereby treating it at par with entities that
  did use the service, is discriminatory and arbitrary.

+ *Alternative remedy is not an absolute bar.* The existence of an
  arbitration clause or a civil remedy does not oust Article 226 where
  discrimination is pleaded.

+ *Limitation and laches.* Relief in writ jurisdiction may be curtailed to
  the period that would have been available in a civil suit, so that the
  writ route does not become a means of circumventing limitation.

+ *Regulated pricing as context.* The fact that the MoPNG fixed the price
  of natural gas was material: it removed the commercial justification for
  a further charge, since GAIL could not claim the charge as part of a
  freely negotiated price.

= Energy Transition and Decarbonisation
<energy-transition-and-decarbonisation>

India's NDC commits to reducing carbon intensity by 45% by 2030 and
achieving net zero by 2070. Policies directed at the oil and gas sector
include:

+ *National Green Hydrogen Mission.* Incentivises the production and use
  of green hydrogen, aiming to decarbonise the energy sector, reduce
  reliance on imported fossil fuels, and develop domestic manufacturing
  capability.

+ *Perform, Achieve, Trade (PAT) Scheme.* A market-based regulatory tool
  to improve sector-wise energy efficiency; the energy sector was included
  in 2015.

+ *SATAT Scheme.* An umbrella scheme uniting various ministries in a
  "whole of government" approach, targeting production of Compressed
  Bio-Gas (CBG) from municipal, sugar industry and agricultural waste.

+ *Ethanol Blended Petrol (EBP) Programme.* Promotes blending ethanol with
  petrol; public sector Oil Marketing Companies are incentivised to sell
  ethanol-blended petrol.

+ *Pradhan Mantri JI-VAN Yojana.* Provides financial assistance to
  second-generation bio-ethanol projects.

+ *CBG blending.* Guidelines have been introduced for blending CBG with
  CNG in city gas distribution networks, and the National Biofuels
  Coordination Committee has set a phase-wise mandatory blending target
  beginning in FY 2025--26.

= The Oilfields (Regulation and Development) Amendment Act 2025
<ord-amendment-2025>

== Background and Legislative History
<ord-amendment-background>

The ORD Act 1948 is the principal legislation governing the exploration and
development of mineral oil resources in India. It was first enacted as the
*Mines and Minerals (Regulation and Development) Act 1948*, a common
statute governing mines and minerals as well as mineral oil resources
(including petroleum and natural gas). When the *Mines and Minerals
(Development and Regulation) Act 1957* was enacted to govern the mining
sector separately, the 1948 Act was renamed the Oilfields (Regulation and
Development) Act and its scope narrowed to oilfields and petroleum.

Crucially, although the statute was renamed, *much of its terminology
continued to reflect the earlier conflation of mining and petroleum
activities*. This is the mischief the 2025 Amendment addresses.

The Amendment Act was introduced as a draft bill in early 2025, passed both
Houses of Parliament, and received the President's assent on *28 March
2025*. It came into force on *15 April 2025*.

Its stated objective, per the Minister of Petroleum and Natural Gas, is to
meet investor expectations, promote ease of doing business, and promote
collaboration between government and contractors. The wider policy driver
is import dependency: India's reliance on imported crude rose to *88.2% in
FY 2024--25*, up from 87.8% the previous year, with oil demand projected to
grow at 3--5% annually through 2030.

== Delinking Petroleum from Mining
<delinking-petroleum-mining>

The most notable change is the dissociation of petroleum operations from
mining operations, eliminating the long-standing practice of grouping both
under a single regulatory framework. This is achieved by introducing a
*Petroleum Lease* in place of a Mining Lease, with the nomenclature changed
across the Act.

The rationale is substantive, not merely terminological: petroleum
exploration and mining differ fundamentally in both method and impact. The
practical mischief was that using the same term *delayed petroleum
exploration operations by requiring irrelevant permits and licences*.

This also removes the requirement of applying twice to government --- once
for a licence and again for a lease --- by introducing a single definition
of "petroleum lease" applicable to both exploration and development.

== Expanded Definition of "Mineral Oils"
<expanded-mineral-oils>

Under the Original Act, "mineral oils" covered only natural gas and
petroleum. The Amendment Act broadens the definition to encompass *all
naturally occurring hydrocarbons*, while expressly clarifying that mineral
oils do *not* include *coal, lignite or helium*.

Notably, it expressly includes unconventional hydrocarbons within the ambit
of Section 3: *shale gas, tight oil, coal bed methane and gas hydrates*.

This is the provision that gives *express statutory recognition to the
uniform licensing concept* which until now existed only as a policy-based
framework under HELP. The Amendment thus lends legislative backing to
India's broader energy objectives, and establishes a legal framework for
commercial exploration and production of shale gas, facilitating foreign
investment in an unexplored resource.

#strong[Shale gas potential]: ONGC estimates 187.5 trillion cubic feet of
shale gas across five basins --- Cambay onland, Ganga Valley, Assam and
Assam Arakan, Krishna-Godavari onland, and Cauvery onland. Deposits are
also estimated in the Cambay, Krishna, Godavari and Kaveri basins. The
United States' shale gas revolution, driven by hydraulic fracturing, is the
stated comparator.

#strong[The unresolved tension]: the Amendment opens the door to shale gas
development, but it remains to be seen how India balances extraction with
the environmental concerns associated with hydraulic fracturing
("fracking"), the primary extraction method.

== Statutory Assurance of Stability: Section 5(3)
<statutory-stability>

The E&P sector is characterised by intensive capital investment, volatile
oil prices, long gestation periods and inherent risk that investment proves
unproductive. Agreements are long-term, making the *perception* of
stability a foremost consideration for investors.

Section 5(3) of the Amendment Act provides:

#quote[
  The terms and conditions of a petroleum lease shall remain stable during
the period of the lease for expeditious and efficient development of
oilfields or production of mineral oils and shall not be altered to the
disadvantage of the lessee during the period of the lease.
]

The provision places a *positive obligation* on the Government to ensure
stability of lease terms, and has been inserted to redress one of the
biggest grievances of global oil companies investing in India.

=== Statutory Stability versus Contractual Stabilisation Clauses

This distinction is analytically important. Both mechanisms aim to mitigate
political and regulatory risk for foreign investors, but their scope
differs:

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: (auto, 1fr, 1fr),
    align: (left, left, left),
    stroke: 0.5pt,
    inset: 6pt,
    table.header([*Aspect*], [*Contractual stabilisation clause*],
      [*Section 5(3)*]),
