=== The Investment Treaty Analogy

A breach of Section 5(3) may be analogised to an investor's claim of breach
of *legitimate expectations* under the *fair and equitable treatment (FET)*
standard in investment treaty arbitration. Where a statutory assurance of
stability is explicitly stated, it may give rise to legitimate expectations
on the part of the contractor. Since the contractor enters the lease relying
on continuity of the regulatory and contractual environment, a unilateral
alteration affecting the tenure of the lease or the viability of the
investment may violate those expectations.

However, India's recent bilateral investment treaties contain *explicit
carve-outs for regulatory measures adopted in the legitimate exercise of
sovereign authority*. These clarify that the mere adverse impact of such
measures on an investor's expectations shall not, by itself, amount to a
breach of the treaty (see India--UAE BIT 2024, Article 3).

== Dispute Resolution: Section 5(2)(e)
<dispute-resolution-s5-2-e>

The Amendment Act inserts Section 5(2)(e), promoting alternative dispute
resolution mechanisms such as arbitration for disputes connected to the
grant or authorisation of petroleum leases. Notably, it *contemplates the
choice of a foreign-seated arbitration* to resolve disputes between the
Government and the contractor.

This is significant because the *model Revenue Sharing Contract designates
a domestic seat* in its dispute resolution clause. The provision therefore
comes as a welcome clarification, augmenting party autonomy in the choice
of seat and boosting investor confidence.

#strong[The contrast with public procurement]: this amendment sits against a
more restrictive approach adopted by the Government in domestic public
procurement. In June 2024, the Ministry of Finance circulated an office
memorandum containing guidelines for arbitration and mediation in domestic
public procurement contracts, imposing restrictions on the use of
arbitration in high-value domestic procurement contracts. The divergence is
instructive of a *targeted, sector-specific approach* rather than a
one-size-fits-all policy: because the focus in E&P is on attracting foreign
investment, the Government has statutorily recognised the possibility of a
foreign seat to match investor expectations.

== Penalties and Adjudication: Revised Section 9
<penalties-section-9>

Section 9 of the Original Act provided that rules made under the Act could
impose penalties --- fines up to INR 1,000, imprisonment up to six months,
or both --- with continued fines for violations continuing after a first
conviction.

The Amendment Act overhauls this provision:

+ *Criminal penalties abolished.* Imprisonment is done away with and
  replaced by stricter financial penalties, imposed by an *Adjudicating
  Authority* designated by the Central Government.

+ *Triggering contraventions.* Penalties attach to contraventions of
  *Section 4A* (barring petroleum operations except under a valid lease in
  accordance with the Act) and *Sections 6A(1) and 6A(2)* (payment of
  royalties by the holder of a petroleum lease in respect of mineral oils).

+ *Quantum.* Up to *INR 25 lakh* for violation of the said provisions, with
  continued infractions attracting fines of up to *INR 10 lakh per day*.
  Rule contraventions attract civil penalties up to INR 25 lakh.

+ *Procedure --- Section 9A.* No penalty may be levied without affording an
  opportunity of being heard. The Adjudicating Authority is not below the
  rank of *Joint Secretary* to the Government of India, and has power to
  summon and enforce the attendance of any person acquainted with the facts,
  and to require production of documents or evidence.

+ *Appeal --- Section 9B.* Appeals against the Adjudicating Authority's
  order lie to the Appellate Tribunal designated under the PNGRB Act 2006,
  that is, *APTEL*.

#strong[The critical observation]: while the Amendment stipulates stricter
financial penalties, it has eliminated criminal penalty entirely. This may
boost investor confidence, but the *absence of criminal liability could
dilute the deterrent effect*, potentially allowing oil and gas majors to
view fines as merely the cost of doing business.

== Unitisation and Environmental Rule-Making
<unitisation-and-environment>

The union government's expanded rule-making powers permit:

+ *Alternative dispute resolution* in the context of leases, including
  seats of arbitration both inside and outside India;
+ *unitisation* of oil and gas fields even across states, so that reservoirs
  extending into two different leases can be developed efficiently; and
+ *enhancing, protecting and conserving the environment*.

The rule-making powers also indicate that the Government intends to lay down
a legislative framework for *comprehensive energy projects*, covering both
oil fields and renewable sources.

== The Draft Petroleum and Natural Gas Rules 2025
<draft-png-rules-2025>

Building on the Amendment, the Ministry of Petroleum and Natural Gas has
released the *Draft Petroleum and Natural Gas Rules 2025* for public
consultation. These Draft Rules will operationalise the Amendment Act, and
their analysis is the subject of the second part of the commentary from
which this section is drawn.

== Why the Amendment Matters
<why-ord-amendment-matters>

+ *Legislative consolidation of policy.* The Amendment embeds long-standing
  policy decisions --- uniform licensing, revenue sharing, open acreage ---
  in statute, converting what was policy-based into legislative obligation.

+ *The delinking rationale.* The rename from Mining Lease to Petroleum
  Lease is not cosmetic: it removes the practical burden of irrelevant
  mining permits that delayed petroleum operations.

+ *Stability as an investment instrument.* Section 5(3) is a statutory
  stabilisation guarantee, but a deliberately narrow one. Its limits matter
  as much as its promise: it does not freeze the wider legal regime, and is
  unlikely to fetter the State's regulatory power.

+ *Sector-specific arbitration policy.* Section 5(2)(e) permits a foreign
  seat, in deliberate contrast to the restrictive approach in domestic
  public procurement --- evidence of a targeted rather than uniform policy.

+ *Deterrence traded for investment.* Abolishing criminal liability removes
  a deterrent in exchange for investor comfort, raising the question whether
  financial penalties alone will secure compliance from large operators.

+ *Unconventional hydrocarbons.* Express statutory inclusion of shale gas,
  tight oil, CBM and gas hydrates provides the legal foundation for their
  commercial development, though the fracking-environment tension remains
  unresolved.

